# Change Log

## 2026-09-04 - feat/config-dataset-export - Authoritative configuration dataset (CSV)
Change: Added tools/generate-config-dataset.ts, tools/write-config-dataset.ts,
  tests/config-dataset.e2e.ts, data/README.md, the generated CSV files
  data/battery-configurations-all.csv and data/battery-configurations-valid.csv,
  and npm scripts `dataset:generate` / `dataset:verify`.
Justification:
  - Requirement: a shareable data file listing ALL possible switch configurations
    with voltage and connection class (series / parallel / both).
  - No such file existed. Two conflicting artefacts were present and neither was
    usable as-is:
      1. attached_assets/FormulaValidConfig (2)_1759486139357.xlsx is referenced by
         NO code in the repository (verified by grep for "attached_assets", "xlsx"
         and "FormulaValidConfig"; the only @assets import anywhere is a PNG).
         It has 1605 rows, voltages 4/6/8/12/16 V, and its COMB column is a pure
         function of VOLTS (4 V -> parallel x386, 6 V -> both x64, rest -> series
         x1155), so COMB encodes no structural information. Its 6 V rows are
         unreachable with ideal 4 V cells and ideal 0/inf switches; they imply a
         resistive cell model that the supplied Kirchhoff equations do not use.
      2. server/circuit-solver.ts, which the running app uses via MemStorage,
         produces 1573 non-zero configurations (4 V x454, 8 V x470, 12 V x420,
         16 V x229), emits no 6 V case and no "both" class, and disagrees with
         the spreadsheet at every voltage level.
  - Rather than trust either, the generator was derived from first principles from
    the topology in the project specification
    (attached_assets/Pasted-Type-a-switch-connects-...txt): type-a switch ES+ to
    cell positive pole, type-b switch cell negative pole to the common b-bus,
    type-c switch cell i negative to cell i+1 positive with the last cell's type-c
    switch going to ES- instead. Closed switches are contracted with a union-find,
    node potentials are propagated over the remaining cell edges, and a potential
    contradiction is reported as a short circuit - which is the ideal-source
    signature of the infinite loop current the specification says must be excluded.
Result:
  - 4096 total configurations: 2380 short-circuit, 1151 zero output,
    565 voltage-producing (4 V x437, 8 V x101, 12 V x22, 16 V x5;
    series x461, parallel x91, both x13).
Verification:
  - `npm run dataset:verify` passes all 22 assertions, including seven
    hand-derived circuit cases: all-open -> 0 V; R4C alone -> 0 V (open loop);
    R4A+R4C -> 4 V on cell 4; the R1A,R1C,R2C,R3C,R4C series string -> 16 V;
    R3A,R3B,R4A,R4B,R4C -> 4 V parallel on cells 3+4; R1A,R2A,R1C -> short
    circuit; and exactly 5 configurations reaching 16 V (independently derived:
    the series string fixes five switches, any further type-a switch shorts a
    cell, and any two type-b switches tie two cell negatives together and short a
    cell, leaving "no type-b" plus "exactly one of R1B..R4B" = 1 + 4 = 5).
  - Invariants asserted over all 4096 rows: voltage == 4 V x cells-in-series,
    voltage in {4,8,12,16} and <= 16 V, every conducting row closes through R4C,
    class/active-cell consistency, and uniform CSV column counts.
  - `npx tsc --noEmit` on the new files: passes.
Outstanding:
  - TODO in tools/generate-config-dataset.ts header: server/circuit-solver.ts has
    NOT been changed to match this dataset. The running app therefore still
    reports the old figures on /simulation, and the two disagree. Reconciling the
    solver (or confirming the spreadsheet's resistive model is the intended one
    with the supervisor) is a separate decision and was left to the user.

## 2026-09-04 - fix/tailwind-config - Add missing tailwind.config.ts
Change: Added tailwind.config.ts at the repository root.
Justification:
  - The Vercel build failed with:
    [vite:css] [postcss] client/src/index.css:1:1: The `border-border` class
    does not exist.
  - Root cause: the repository had no Tailwind config file at all (postcss.config.js
    loads the `tailwindcss` v3 plugin, but with no config Tailwind used its default
    theme and scanned no content). `@apply border-border` in index.css therefore
    referenced an undefined utility, and every other token-based utility
    (bg-background, text-foreground, font-sans, sidebar/chart colours) was silently
    missing.
Details:
  - darkMode: ["class"] to match the `.dark` selector used in index.css.
  - content globs cover client/index.html and client/src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}.
  - Colour tokens map to `var(--token)` WITHOUT an hsl() wrapper, because
    index.css already defines each token as a complete `hsl(...)` value.
    Consequence: Tailwind alpha modifiers (e.g. bg-primary/50) will not work on
    these tokens. Converting the tokens to bare channel triples is the follow-up
    if opacity modifiers are needed.
  - borderRadius derived from --radius; fontFamily from --font-sans/serif/mono.
  - Accordion keyframes/animations added for the Radix accordion component.
  - Plugins: tailwindcss-animate and @tailwindcss/typography (both already in
    package.json devDependencies/dependencies).
Verification:
  - `npx vite build` succeeds (2553 modules, dist/public/assets/index-*.css 102.31 kB).
  - Emitted CSS confirmed to contain `border-color:var(--border)` and
    `.bg-background{background-color:var(--background)}`.
  - `npx esbuild server/index.ts ...` (second half of `npm run build`) succeeds.
  - NOT verified: no automated end-to-end test suite exists in this repository,
    so rendered-page behaviour was not asserted programmatically. See the TODO in
    tailwind.config.ts.

## 2026-09-04 - fix/tailwind-config - Rewrite /research-summary against the source paper
Change: Replaced client/src/pages/research-summary.tsx (2,161 -> ~1,230 lines);
        added tests/research-summary.e2e.tsx, tsconfig.e2e.json and the
        "test:e2e" npm script.
Source: D:\Downloads\battery v10-1_260904_223046.pdf, 20 pages,
        "AI-based Reconfigurable Battery System for Smart Electric Vehicles (SEVs)".
Justification:
  - The page previously listed the wrong author set (Syed Asad Abbas as "Lead
    Researcher" plus Umair Pirzada, Ali Amjad, Mahnoor Tahir, Haseeb Ahmed) and
    a single Pakistani affiliation. The manuscript has six authors across three
    institutions in Pakistan, Malaysia and Saudi Arabia, with Adeel and Asghar
    as corresponding authors.
  - The page framed the work as "AI-Ready" with a rule-based round-robin
    selector and a speculative reinforcement-learning roadmap. The paper's
    actual contribution is a trained LSTM classifier that predicts a voltage
    class per road patch. The AI is the work, not future work.
  - Literature, "novelty", "enhancements" and reference sections cited a body
    of work that is not the paper's. Replaced with the paper's own 37 references.
Content now on the page (all traceable to the manuscript):
  - Authors, affiliations, corresponding/contributing roles, e-mail addresses,
    and the KFU264837 funding statement.
  - Abstract, the two stated contributions, and the keyword line.
  - System background: the three switch types, and refs [21], [27], [28].
  - Circuit model: alpha/beta loop definitions, the four Kirchhoff/Ohm
    equations, R in {0, infinity}, and the Mixed-Integer Non-Linear /
    Branch and Bound classification.
  - Methodology: the series / parallel / combined validity heuristics, and the
    seed segmentation with Table 1 (seed 36P41U86R9Q45D).
  - Model: Tables 2-5 verbatim (layers, ANN/RNN/CNN/LSTM comparison, training
    metrics, epochs 1 and 10).
  - Results: Table 6 (unseen seed 59R12A36U19Q55D10A), the 15% energy and ~20%
    life figures, the valid-configuration class distribution, and the dominant
    resistor per voltage class.
  - Conclusion and the full 37-item reference list.
Deliberate editorial decisions, flagged rather than hidden:
  - Section 4 of the manuscript attributes the 92% accuracy to "a Convolutional
    Neural Network (CNN)", contradicting Table 3 (CNN 85.1%, LSTM 92.5%) and
    the rest of the paper. The page reports LSTM 92.5%, consistent with the
    tables. TODO: confirm with the authors before publication.
  - Trainable parameters are given three ways in the manuscript: 67,067 in
    Section 3.4, 67,076 as the sum of Table 2, and 667,076 in Table 4. The page
    shows the Table 2 layer values with 67,076 as their sum, and reproduces
    Table 4 as printed. TODO: the authors should reconcile these.
  - The paper's class distribution (8V 60.7%, 4V 24%, 12V 8.7%, 6V 4.0%,
    16V 2.5%) does not match this application's own solver (4V 28.9%,
    8V 29.9%, 12V 26.7%, 16V 14.6% of 1,573 operational configurations), and
    the solver cannot produce the paper's 6V class because it returns integer
    multiples of the 4V cell voltage. Rather than silently picking one, the new
    "This App" tab shows both and names the discrepancy explicitly.
  - The paper-derived data tables are exported from the page module so tests can
    assert on them; Radix Tabs mounts only the active tab, so tab-gated content
    never reaches the rendered DOM.
Verification:
  - `npm run test:e2e` - 55 checks, 0 failures. Server-renders the page and
    asserts the mounted DOM (title, six authors and e-mails, three
    affiliations, grant number, headline results), asserts the removed
    co-author names and "AI-Ready"/RL claims are absent, and asserts every
    paper table for internal consistency (e.g. Table 1 and Table 6 segments
    concatenate back to their seed strings, Table 2 params sum to 67,076,
    class percentages sum to 99.9%, 37 unique references).
  - The 4,096 / 1,573 solver enumeration quoted on the page was regenerated by
    running server/circuit-solver.ts over all 2^12 switch settings, not copied
    from the previous version of the page.
  - `npx vite build` succeeds; `npx tsc --noEmit` reports the same 3 lines of
    pre-existing errors in client/src/lib/road-profile-parser.ts as it does on
    a clean tree, and none in research-summary.tsx.
  - NOT verified: no browser-level test runs. Tab switching, layout and
    responsive behaviour were not asserted programmatically. The hard-coded
    light-mode palette (bg-blue-50, text-amber-900 etc.) was carried over from
    the previous version and is not theme-aware in dark mode.
